   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law, in relation to revising the standards relating to the board of directors of domestic life insurance companies",
    "basePrintNo": "S3012",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3012-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1202, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3012\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\nrevising the standards relating to the board of directors of domestic\nlife insurance companies\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will amend § 1202(b) of the Insurance Law to authorize a\nparent insurance corporation and other entities to satisfy the\nindependent board of director requirements of such subsection for\nsubsidiary, domestic life insurance corporations\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends § 1202(b)(3) of the insurance law to authorize parent\ninsurance corporations, mutual insurance holding companies, or a\npublicly held corporation to fulfill the independent board of director\nrequirements of § 1202(b) of the Insurance Law for domestic life\ninsurers. Current law limits which entity within a holding system may\nperform the independent director functions of a domestic life insurer\nto only domestic subsidiaries if the parent company is also a domestic\nlife insurance company.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSection 1202(b) of the Insurance Law establishes the rules for\nindependent members of the Board of Directors of domestic life\ninsurance corporations in New York. Paragraph (1) of this subsection\nrequires that the board of directors of such insurers and each\ncommittee of the board must be made up of not less than one-third\nindependent members (persons who are not officers or employees of such\ninsurer or of any entity controlled by or controlling such insurer)\nAdditionally, paragraph (2) of this subsection provides that such\ninsurer must establish one or more committees comprised solely of\nindependent directors to perform the functions of an audit committee,\ncompensation committee and nominating committee.\n\nUnder current New York law, these independent board member\nrequirements can only be satisfied within the Board of Directors of\nthe domestic life insurer, the only authorized exception being for\ncircumstances where the domestic life insurer is controlled by another\ndomestic life insurer that is in compliance with these requirements.\n\nThese provisions of § 1202(b) of the Insurance Law are consistent with\nthe requirements for independent audit committees set forth in the\nAnnual Financial Reporting Model Regulation (\"Model Audit Rule\") of\nthe National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC) and the\nrequirements for independent board members, nominating and\ncompensation committees set forth in the NAIC Insurance Holding\nCompany System Regulatory Model Act (\"Holding Company Act\"). The\nindependent Board requirements of these Models are virtually identical\nto the New York law but for the fact that the Models authorize the\nindependent Board functions to be performed by parent corporations\nwithin a holding company system The Models recognize the efficiencies\nof performing these independent Board functions at a parent company\nlevel on behalf of multiple affiliated insurers.\n\n\nTaken together, for a holding company doing business nationwide, this\nresults in circumstances where the entity is able to satisfy\napplicable requirements for independent board members at a parent\nholding company level in every state, other than New York Holding\ncompanies that have a New York domestic life insurance subsidiary must\nperform these functions separately at the New York domestic insurer\nlevel.\n\nSince the enactment of the federal Sarbanes Oxley Act, which imposed\nstringent standards and additional liability for the Board of\nDirectors of business corporations, it has been increasingly difficult\nto attract and retain individuals to serve in that function,\nespecially at the domestic subsidiary company level.  Individuals\nmaking the commitment to serve as Board members at the parent holding\ncompany level often have more, higher quality business experience than\nthose individuals choosing to serve at a single state subsidiary level\nAdditionally, since 2008, business corporate governance standards have\nreflected a growing reliance on and requirements for oversight of the\nrisks inherent to the entire holding company enterprise, rather than\neach individual entity component All of these factors substantiate the\nnational standard for independent directors reflected in the current\nNAIC Models, rather than the outdated provisions of the current New\nYork law.\n\nIn addition to being totally inconsistent with the national standard\nfor independent directors, this provision of New York law is also\nunfair in that it only applies to domestic life insurers and does not\nextend to domestic property-casualty insurers.\n\nThe proposed amendment will bring New York law into compliance with\nthe independent audit committee requirements of the Model Audit Rule\nin all states and the independent board member, compensation and\nnominating committee requirements of the Holding Company Act in the\nstates which have adopted the independent director requirements.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediately.\n\n",
